  "strategy": "Approach and rationale  \nThe target is to improve upon the current smallest overlap integral bound found so far (0.3808). The current best solution is 0.380865, and we aim to push it below 0.3808. The optimization of the overlap integral is non-convex, and the binary representation approach used in the previous code tends to create discontinuities that may not yield the globally minimal solution. We introduce a smoothed step function with continuous values and optimize using randomized search to avoid local traps. The new strategy focuses on:\n\n1. A continuous function representation (mid-values between samples) to allow dense feature placement  \n2. Randomized function synthesis with a specific pattern generation rather than local swaps  \n3. Comparing function intervals rather than direct correlation terms to reduce dimensionality  \n4. Factor scaling of frequency spacing to balance concentration costs  \n5. Score aggregation across multiple scales through multiplicative components  \n\nThe rationale is that a continuous representation with randomized frequency factors allows better optimization than binary structures, and by generating candidates through parameterized oscillations rather than swaps, we remain computationally efficient while exploring a wider design space.\n\nImplementation structure:\n1. Function construction using frequency-based mid-value computation  \n2. Validity checks and integral normalization  \n3. Score calculation using interval-based correlation  \n4. Randomized search with factors controlling scale and amplitudes  \n5. Multiplicative score scaling and individual/large-scale weighting  \n6. Temperature-based termination and fallback procedures  \n\nThe implementation focuses on producing valid feature functions satisfying the sum constraint through explicit normalization. Optimization proceeds by considering trade-offs between peak concentration and spread relaxation using contrast and frequency factors.\n\nMathematical specification  \nGiven domain [0,2], we define functions through:  \n- Normalization: $ $\\frac{1}{2} \\sum_{i} f_i \\Delta x = 1 $ $  \n- Validity: $0 \\le f_i \\le 1$  \n\nScore:  \n$F(x) = \\max_{k} \\left( \\iint_F h(x) \\cdot (1-h(x+k)) dx \\right) \\cdot \\Delta x$\n\nWe express this using interval-overlap counting and approximate through:\n- $\\text{SCORE} = \\max_{\\text{interval}} \\left( \\text{INITIAL\\_OVERLAP} + \\epsilon \\cdot \\text{L1\\_CHANGE} \\right)$\n- Weighted by scale-distortions via alpha and beta exponents to penalize abrupt changes\n\nAlgorithm overview:\n- Generate candidate from amplitude (A) and frequency (B) factors\n- Construct points through trigonometric oscillations applied to mid-values  \n- Check validity and normalize integral  \n- Compute-score aggregate from both local and global gradients  \n- Return best candidate found within time window  \n\nImplementation details:\n1. Functions are constructed via:\n   h_values = 0.5 + \\eta \\cdot \\sin(2\\pi B \\cdot i \\cdot dx + \\phi)\n   where $\\eta = A \\cdot \\tanh(\\gamma)$\n\n2. Integral normalization:\n   current_sum = sum(h_values) * dx\n   h_values *= 1 / current_sum\n\n3. Score calculation:\n   corr <- np.correlate(h_values, 1-h_values, mode='full')\n   score = max(corr) * dx * alpha + score_consistency * beta\n\n4. Parameters:\n   - B in [3.82, 4.00] (frequency factors spaced by 0.03)\n   - A in [0.68, 0.80] (amplitudes scaled by 0.95)\n   - Random perturbations enabled via phi in [0,2pi]\n\n5. Optimization constraints:\n   - enforce_validity(h_values) ensures 0<=h<=1\n   - balance_integral(h_values) normalizes\n\nSearch procedure:\n- Progressive refinement: start with small factor shifts then expand\n- Local improvement: repair via magnitude clamping if necessary\n- Early termination if score improvements stagnate\n\nCompute budget:\n- Target 200 candidates/second with fallback to pattern-based generation\n- Maximum 1000 evaluations:\n   - Median time/query \u2248 0.5s\n   - Total time <~500s\n\nValidation:\n- Score = max_k(overlap integrated) * dx\n- Fallback solution always valid (binary step function)\n- All outputs checked for integral and bound compliance",
